{
  "file": "crates/habeas-sdk/tests/storage.rs",
  "item": "impl[StoredGraph/Domain]/fn[outgoing_edges]",
  "citations": [
    {
      "role": "cite",
      "name": "habeas:coverage",
      "commentary": "Domain requires this callback even for a storage-only consumer. The\n delegation test must use only node_path; discovering edges would be\n an unwanted graph access, which this guard rejects. Traversal fixtures\n separately exercise edge discovery."
    }
  ],
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "The `habeas:coverage` citation on `crates/habeas-sdk/tests/storage.rs#impl[StoredGraph/Domain]/fn[outgoing_edges]` is in the permitted form: fenced commentary on an explicit function with a body. The function is a guard that panics, so a passing test never executes it. That makes the zero-execution exception relevant.\n\nThe reason it gives is substantive and not just difficulty. `Domain` requires the callback. `StoredGraph` is a storage-only consumer. The delegation test, `consumer_delegates_evidence_storage_using_only_the_requested_nodes_path`, and the other storage tests use only `node_path`, `load_evidence` and `store_evidence`, which delegate to `FileEvidenceStorage`. Discovering edges there would be an unwanted graph access, and the panic guard rejects it.\n\nThe explanation agrees with the current source. The body panics with \"storage operations must not discover graph edges\", and `StoredReview::execute` never touches edges. The claim that traversal fixtures exercise edge discovery also holds. Other fixtures implement `outgoing_edges` with real logic, for example `Application/Domain` in `tests/contracts.rs` and `Memory/Domain` in `tests/validation.rs`.\n\nThe citation is not redundant, because the item carries no {impl} or {test} reference to the same name.",
